    On the screen behind the lectern, the climbing number, 61,408,
blinked once.
    Vale lifted a hand.
    “Neighbors,” he said, kindly, into the microphone. “A moment.”
    He did not seem angry. He seemed, if anything, interested. He
looked at Papo and Wilson, at the broken count, at the big man in the
ill-fitting tuxedo drumming softly on his knees, with the expression of
a student who’s been given an unexpected problem and finds that he
likes it.
    He took out his notebook.
    He wrote one line, and nodded, to himself, at something only he
could see.
    I didn’t understand it until later. He wasn’t surprised by the seven.
He was noting it. Somewhere in a page I’d never seen, in a column I
couldn’t read, a man in a gray coat had just written down the parti‐
cipants’ response to polyrhythmic disruption, with a time, and a note about
what to try next.



At 9:34, the house lights dropped another notch and a new sound
entered the hall.
   It came from the staff.
   All around the walls, in the shadows between the curtains, men and
women in gray polo shirts, ushers, attendants, a supervisor with a ra‐
dio, stood with their hands clasped, and in each of their ears, I saw
now, was a small, flesh-colored bud. A click. A metronome, so quiet I
felt it in my teeth. They were moving, in unison, like a single animal.




